00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:18.220
to constantly evolve
with us, but they

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:19.990
can adopt as they need to.

00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:23.440
We want to enable, not
disrupt our customers.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:26.680
But at the same time, we
need to give them more, give

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:30.982
our consumers more, on
a minute-by-minute basis

00:08:30.982 --> 00:08:32.440
and not say, oh,
you've got to wait

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:35.140
six months to get something.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.080
And so, there's a lot
of aspects to that.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:40.809
So we sat down, and
the first thing we did

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:46.150
is developed an idea of
a microservices-based

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:47.200
architecture.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.330
We have a lot of
commonality around the globe

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:50.720
of how we do things.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.370
And so we said, well, let's
break those capabilities

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:59.210
into discrete microservice--
you remember Matt's slide--

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:02.120
which we call our core
services framework.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.660
It takes discrete
capabilities, moves those.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.640
And then, how do
we then have those

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.740
be system-to-system
conversations as opposed

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:14.310
to human-to-system
conversations, et cetera?

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:17.567
And so we said,
OK, well, got it.

00:09:17.567 --> 00:09:18.150
Microservices.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:18.690
Perfect.

00:09:18.690 --> 00:09:20.590
Makes sense.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:21.250
Capabilities.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:22.120
We aligned to those.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:27.020
We have tons of them that
we're breaking into those.

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.620
We said, well, we've got to have
a way to connect all of that.

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:36.440
We've got to have a way
to implement that strategy

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.670
on a global scale and focus
on the capabilities that

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:43.160
drive that value.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:46.130
How do we develop and deploy
those reusable microservices?

00:09:46.130 --> 00:09:51.140
How do we let both internal
and external consumers--

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:53.390
meaning our dev teams, our
product groups, our product

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:55.460
alliances, our customers--
take those things,

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:59.000
stitch them together to
build the capabilities

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:00.650
that they need?

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:03.960
Do it without requiring tons
of customization of code

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:06.920
but really configurable
capabilities.

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:11.150
Create a consistent
experience across all that.

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:13.970
Simplify the formats.

00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:16.162
Make data available whether
you want to consume it.

00:10:16.162 --> 00:10:17.370
You might want to consume it.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:18.830
You might not want
to consume it.

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:23.510
Do it all in a governed manner.

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:26.830
You can imagine, if any of are
in fintech, regulatory issues,

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.822
we've got them out the wazoo.

00:10:29.822 --> 00:10:31.280
And so we have to
have systems that

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:37.510
allow for that discrete
management of those policies.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:38.875
Reduce the response time.

00:10:38.875 --> 00:10:41.000
So on getting data.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:45.554
You think about your
stack that includes

00:10:45.554 --> 00:10:49.077
13 hops to get to root
data and everything that

00:10:49.077 --> 00:10:50.160
has to be done in-between.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:53.120
You're talking about some
substantial transaction time.

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:55.720
So how do we reduce that?

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:57.910
How do we then also take
all of these things,

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:01.210
reduce the risk on our
back-end stability?

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:03.910
And how we do it with
security from the ground up,

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.852
built in, not bolted on?

00:11:06.852 --> 00:11:08.560
So we turned all those
into requirements.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:10.893
We said, OK, well, that's our
requirements for how we're

00:11:10.893 --> 00:11:17.290
going to do this thing with APIs
and stitching our microservices

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:19.480
together so that
ultimately we can

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.330
reduce that time to integration,
improve our speed to market,

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:25.720
have that faster response time,
lower our maintenance cost,

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:29.110
have shorter feedback
loops, have more rapidly

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:33.820
available capabilities to the
marketplace, both internally

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:37.990
and externally, eating our
own dog food, if you will.

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:40.818
So we said, well,
OK, well, we've

00:11:40.818 --> 00:11:42.610
got to create a framework
for our strategy.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:49.780
We said, well, OK, first is
establish a digital strategy.

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:51.610
We've did that.

00:11:51.610 --> 00:11:54.140
We aligned our
organization and culture.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:59.530
So we were very, very monolithic
in our architecture and very

00:11:59.530 --> 00:12:03.280
monolithic across
multiple monoliths,

00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:07.230
if you will, in the way our
technology teams organize.

00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:08.390
We said, you know what?

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:11.410
If we're going to go with a--
instead of following Conway,

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:15.160
we're going to beat him
over the head a little bit

00:12:15.160 --> 00:12:16.407
and say, listen.

00:12:16.407 --> 00:12:18.490
If we're going to build
this around microservices,

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:20.890
let's build our teams
and dedicate teams

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:23.330
to owning and building
those microservices,

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.720
build it, own it, operate
it, run true SRE model

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:27.670
for everything we're doing.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.825
Total culture shift
for the company.

00:12:30.825 --> 00:12:32.950
Which, of course, you can't
just replace a culture.

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:35.080
You have to guide
it and transform it

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.760
and tweak it and move it along.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:40.810
So that became part
of our strategy.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:45.760
We had to go out and look and
evaluate and build or deploy

00:12:45.760 --> 00:12:47.860
supporting technologies--

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:52.353
GCP, other cloud providers
who will go unnamed.

00:12:52.353 --> 00:12:54.520
The tools that they provide,
whether they're managed

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:58.000
services, whether they're
capabilities where

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:01.390
they're simply run,
infrastructure-as-a-service

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:03.250
type things.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.425
And then we had to engage
our partner ecosystem

00:13:05.425 --> 00:13:07.300
to make sure that we're
doing the right thing

00:13:07.300 --> 00:13:08.613
and learn from each other.

00:13:08.613 --> 00:13:10.030
We're learning
from our customers.

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:11.290
Our customers are
learning from us.

00:13:11.290 --> 00:13:12.340
We're learning
from our partners.

00:13:12.340 --> 00:13:13.840
Our partners are
learning from us.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:18.580
We have Google teams deep in
the bowels of the company who

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:21.020
are embedded and helping
us do a lot of things.

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:24.160
One of those things being, of
course, implementing Apigee.

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:30.670
So that set up our
framework, set up our thing.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:34.680
It says, OK, well, then
what is our strategy?

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:35.950
APIs everywhere.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:36.990
APIs for everything.

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:37.920
Everything is API.

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:46.320
Even internally, we want to
ensure that this service is

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:47.320
talking to that service.

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.360
Everybody's use a common
logging, monitoring,

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:50.340
metering services.

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:51.790
Everybody's using a common this.

00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:54.338
If you need this capability,
that service is discoverable.

00:13:54.338 --> 00:13:55.463
The swaggers are out there.

00:13:55.463 --> 00:13:58.420
You can grab it,
deploy it, run with it.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:00.500
Internally open sourced.

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:03.550
So if you need to extend it, you
can extend it, govern it back

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:08.440
into the company, the team that
owns it, and do all of this

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:09.580
to--

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.950
basically, what it
says up there is

00:14:11.950 --> 00:14:14.590
rapidly enable
those capabilities

00:14:14.590 --> 00:14:16.570
and minimize the investment.

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:19.353
That's what all of our
CFOs are looking for.

00:14:19.353 --> 00:14:20.770
So that's what
we're trying to do.

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:21.940
It's certainly what we want.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:23.770
Makes our lives easier.

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:25.030
Improves our quality of life.

00:14:25.030 --> 00:14:27.220
Improves our quality of
service to our customers.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.220
Improves the quality of
service to our investors

00:14:29.220 --> 00:14:30.330
and our partners.

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:34.450
Will drive high
levels of automation.

00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:38.170
Be able to incorporate
more scope faster.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:41.320
Be a lot more efficient
in how we're doing things.

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:45.800
Be able to personalize those
APIs through configuration.

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:52.880
Be able to adapt as you can,
as you want to, as a customer.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.890
Adapt those changes or not.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:00.450
Where are we?

00:15:00.450 --> 00:15:02.700
Well, we started an API program.

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.450
We defined our
microservices architecture.

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:07.250
We started an API
program to work it.

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:08.373
We started a tribe.

00:15:08.373 --> 00:15:10.040
You call it a center
of excellence here,

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:11.540
but internally, we
call them tribes--

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:13.130
if you're familiar
with Spotify model.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:16.280
We started a tribe, just
actually defining those things.

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:19.700
We have a product alliance,
who owns the product aspect,

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.240
the external aspect of it.

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.780
We have an internal product
thing that is actually

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:25.550
defining, hey, you know what?

00:15:25.550 --> 00:15:31.220
Development Team X, what do
you need out of our capability?

00:15:31.220 --> 00:15:34.910
We started both internal
and partner APIs

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:37.280
so that we can start leveraging,
pushing those globally.

00:15:39.752 --> 00:15:41.710
Went to centralized
governance of these things.

00:15:41.710 --> 00:15:43.860
So instead of everybody getting
to do whatever they want,

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:45.360
whenever they want,
however they want, we said,

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:47.370
well, no, here's some
guardrails, principles.

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:49.350
We don't want to be
overly prescriptive,

00:15:49.350 --> 00:15:52.620
but we also don't want
to be overly hands-off.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:57.412
So you end up with sprawl
and disparate technologies

00:15:57.412 --> 00:15:58.870
and right back to
the problem state

00:15:58.870 --> 00:16:00.790
that we had in the first place.

00:16:00.790 --> 00:16:02.320
So we said, let's not do that.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:04.880
Let's actually have
some governance

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:07.820
around how these
things are done.

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:13.740
Went to drive the adoption
heavily through discovery.

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:16.440
Every tribe, every
microservice, has

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:19.210
a page in our service catalog
says, hey, here's what we do.

00:16:19.210 --> 00:16:20.100
Here's who we are.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:21.700
Here's who are support
infrastructure.

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:22.260
Here's who you call.

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:23.250
Here's who owns it.

00:16:23.250 --> 00:16:24.300
Here's who designed it.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:26.740
Here's the link to
the source code.

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:28.710
Here's the swagger.

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:30.430
Here's some example code.

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:32.710
Here's how you do this.

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:34.522
And that's internally.

00:16:34.522 --> 00:16:35.730
Where are we on that journey?

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:36.810
Well we're down it.

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:38.550
I mean, we've got
a long way to go,

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.470
but that's what we're doing.

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:44.320
And we are making
strong headway in that,

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:47.040
and what that's helping
us do is actually define

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:50.640
that, OK, if we want
our customers to engage,

00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:53.470
whether they're furnishing
data or consuming data from us,

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:57.200
we want them to engage the
same way as a partner ecosystem

00:16:57.200 --> 00:16:58.330
within its developers.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:00.750
So that's helping us
define the capabilities

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:02.130
and the requirements
that we need

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:05.890
and that we have around
our Gateway Platform.

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:11.190
Ultimately, that helps
us transform the customer

00:17:11.190 --> 00:17:14.349
acquisition.

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:16.839
I think we're doing OK.

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:18.310
We've got some early stuff.

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:22.210
And DP's going to give us
a demo here in a short bit.

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:23.560
I think we're doing OK.

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:26.871
We've got the tribes,
rather, that are

00:17:26.871 --> 00:17:28.329
in the product
alliances are really

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:30.060
starting to grasp onto it.

00:17:30.060 --> 00:17:31.660
And it's a struggle
a little bit.

00:17:31.660 --> 00:17:34.150
We've got to operate
the old, keep that

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:35.545
running while we build the new.

00:17:35.545 --> 00:17:39.310
The new, we're not
really evolving code.

00:17:39.310 --> 00:17:44.150
We're completely replacing it,
which makes for a lot of fun.

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:45.830
But it's going OK.

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:47.825
We're pretty excited about it.

00:17:47.825 --> 00:17:50.200
There's a lot of folks in here
from the architecture team

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:52.465
and the technology
leadership team who

00:17:52.465 --> 00:17:55.300
I wish weren't in the room.

00:17:55.300 --> 00:17:58.930
But these are all
folks that are engaged

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:00.610
every minute of
every day in actually

00:18:00.610 --> 00:18:02.550
making this come to life.

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:06.250
And the amount of passion
that we have in doing it

00:18:06.250 --> 00:18:07.300
has been pretty amazing.

00:18:11.770 --> 00:18:13.562
Because we're doing
this on a global scale.

00:18:13.562 --> 00:18:14.062
a?

00:18:14.062 --> 00:18:15.750
Lot of people
don't realize this.

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:16.600
You might all.

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:20.020
But a lot of people don't
realize that Equifax is not

00:18:20.020 --> 00:18:21.370
just a US company.

00:18:21.370 --> 00:18:22.030
We are global.

00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:25.390
We're in 24 countries.

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:27.760
The credit industry
actually exists everywhere

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:30.660
in the world in some
form or fashion.

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:31.660
Sometimes it's positive.

00:18:31.660 --> 00:18:32.660
Sometimes it's negative.

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:34.683
Sometimes just the
way those things work.

00:18:34.683 --> 00:18:36.100
But it actually
exists everywhere,

00:18:36.100 --> 00:18:38.017
and right now we're in
24 of those countries--

00:18:38.017 --> 00:18:43.520
Middle East, Asia, North
America, South America,

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:46.030
Australia, and New
Zealand, Canada.

00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:47.200
We're all over the place.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:54.340
And we're doing things
beyond just credit reports.

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:58.180
We do business intelligence
and employment verification.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:00.323
We do identity and
fraud services.

00:19:00.323 --> 00:19:02.740
We're one of the largest holders
of identity in the world,

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:03.590
believe it or not.

00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:07.488
We have 1.5 billion unique
or something like that.

00:19:07.488 --> 00:19:09.280
I don't know if I put
somebody on the spot.

00:19:09.280 --> 00:19:11.830
I think it's something
like 1.5 billion people

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:13.330
within our scope.

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:17.970
So we're providing
that on a global scale.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.170
Well, so we needed
a solution, we

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:24.590
needed a platform that would
meet that global scale,

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:28.510
it would meet that
availability requirement

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:31.430
on providing access to
all of these services

00:19:31.430 --> 00:19:33.340
and these capabilities,
providing access

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:35.900
to the data in a governed model.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:39.940
And that's where we
settled on Apigee

00:19:39.940 --> 00:19:44.650
and used that as the
core for our framework

00:19:44.650 --> 00:19:51.790
and how we're producing those
services out to the market.

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:54.110
We're running that
all over the world.

00:19:54.110 --> 00:19:55.570
We have multiple regions.

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:59.020
We're running it in
multiple providers.

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:03.120
We are fairly leaning
heavily towards the GCP

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:04.010
stack at this point.

00:20:04.010 --> 00:20:05.910
If anybody read
our earnings call,

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:08.850
I think the most-used
word was Google.

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:11.520
But we have to be multi-cloud.

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:13.800
Certainly everybody
knows Apigee's--

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:15.220
where it came from.

00:20:15.220 --> 00:20:16.350
We have a lot run on AWS.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:17.475
We have a lot running here.

00:20:17.475 --> 00:20:19.000
We have a lot running there.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:20.760
And that's fine.

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:23.430
It adds some
complexity but makes

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:26.220
sure that we're actually
paying attention and doing

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:27.140
things the right way.

00:20:30.307 --> 00:20:32.140
Because this is the
kind of stuff that we're

00:20:32.140 --> 00:20:33.910
producing through it.

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:35.860
These are some of the
things I mentioned

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:38.830
that we have lots of
different types of clients

00:20:38.830 --> 00:20:40.230
of our developer portal.

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:43.362
We have customers,
mobile, web, our service,

00:20:43.362 --> 00:20:44.695
our internal services framework.

00:20:44.695 --> 00:20:47.560
That's the bottom one
that got cut off there.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:51.123
That's our internal
users of the same thing.

00:20:51.123 --> 00:20:52.540
All going through
a gateway, which

00:20:52.540 --> 00:20:57.870
is providing us governance,
authentication, analytics,

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:03.480
monetization, et cetera, in a
framework that is not custom

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:06.090
built. So everybody's
familiar with it

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:09.750
or can be easily
familiar with it.

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:12.990
And we're pushing our swaggers
out all over the place

00:21:12.990 --> 00:21:15.060
so that people can get
it and actually onboard

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:18.750
onto the developer portal DP's
going to show you in a minute,

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:20.925
because we're providing
this type of stuff.

00:21:20.925 --> 00:21:24.510
Like I mentioned, asset,
wealth, verification.

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:27.540
Did anybody know we do all these
things besides-- if you're not

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:28.080
at Equifax?

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:30.420
A lot of people think
we're just credit,

00:21:30.420 --> 00:21:32.516
but we're so far beyond that.

00:21:32.516 --> 00:21:34.290
And so we have to
be able to provide

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:38.360
for a robust methodology
to integrate.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:43.070
Like I said, we're
down that journey.

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:46.610
We publicly launched
our dev portal--

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.460
DP, I don't know
how long ago it was.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:54.150
But we're already onboarded a
number of customers into that.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:55.470
They're starting to use it.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:59.160
We're starting to see some
really great results out of it,

00:21:59.160 --> 00:22:01.830
just as we are today.

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:06.120
We're reducing the design
of some of those APIs

00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:08.250
from things that would
take months because we're

00:22:08.250 --> 00:22:09.815
going a lot more discreet.

00:22:09.815 --> 00:22:11.940
And we're doing this on a
microservices [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:11.940 --> 00:22:14.763
we're down to a week.

00:22:14.763 --> 00:22:16.180
We're reducing
that time-to-market

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:17.700
in a lot of cases.

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:21.120
We've got drastically
improved security.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.340
We were already in good
shape, but it's bolted on.

00:22:25.565 --> 00:22:26.940
Now we're doing
it, and it's just

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:29.700
inherent in what we're doing.

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:32.940
We're enabling our customers
who are self-service portal.

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:34.080
Big deal.

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:34.980
Love our customers.

00:22:34.980 --> 00:22:38.610
Customers love us,
most of the time.

00:22:38.610 --> 00:22:41.130
But they really are
starting-- especially

00:22:41.130 --> 00:22:42.810
in the banking
industry-- they're

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:46.140
starting to move from being,
we'll never go onto the cloud,

00:22:46.140 --> 00:22:48.420
to just like, hey, Equifax,
what are you doing?

00:22:48.420 --> 00:22:50.190
Can you help us?

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:52.590
We're helping them
move down these paths.

00:22:52.590 --> 00:22:55.650
And we have some banks
and some customers--

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:59.310
take the Credit Karmas
and the Cap Ones

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:01.225
and stuff-- that
are far beyond us,

00:23:01.225 --> 00:23:02.850
