00:34:59.360 --> 00:35:02.320
Just put those values in here.

00:35:02.320 --> 00:35:03.820
Make a call.

00:35:03.820 --> 00:35:09.160
This goes to the back-end
system in real-time,

00:35:09.160 --> 00:35:13.960
makes a call to the OAuth
authorization service,

00:35:13.960 --> 00:35:16.820
and it will come
back with a token.

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:18.580
So this is the access
token that I've

00:35:18.580 --> 00:35:20.980
got from the OAuth service.

00:35:20.980 --> 00:35:22.810
Now I just take
this token, and I

00:35:22.810 --> 00:35:26.590
can go ahead and make API
calls to any of these APIs.

00:35:26.590 --> 00:35:29.990
So I'm going to show
you something else here.

00:35:29.990 --> 00:35:35.250
So let me pull this
token API call.

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:36.940
OK.

00:35:36.940 --> 00:35:37.700
I can make a call.

00:35:37.700 --> 00:35:39.700
So this actually sends
you all the response back

00:35:39.700 --> 00:35:41.430
from the sandbox environment.

00:35:41.430 --> 00:35:43.840
So the developer actually
made our request,

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:45.340
sent it all the way
to the back-end,

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:47.090
and got a response back.

00:35:47.090 --> 00:35:50.710
So what I'm going to show you
now is the back-end system.

00:35:50.710 --> 00:35:55.300
So this is our Google
Apigee gateway,

00:35:55.300 --> 00:35:58.660
and what I'm going to show you
is typically what happens is

00:35:58.660 --> 00:36:01.030
when our customers are
testing these APIs,

00:36:01.030 --> 00:36:03.575
either in the sandbox or
in a test environment,

00:36:03.575 --> 00:36:06.200
[? non-production, ?] they have
a lot of these challenges where

00:36:06.200 --> 00:36:07.117
they're having issues.

00:36:07.117 --> 00:36:08.920
They want us to troubleshoot.

00:36:08.920 --> 00:36:11.607
It has been very
challenging for us

00:36:11.607 --> 00:36:13.190
to really troubleshoot
and figure out,

00:36:13.190 --> 00:36:15.203
hey, well, looks like
we have given you

00:36:15.203 --> 00:36:16.870
the right specifications,
but you're not

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:18.410
getting the right response.

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:20.050
So a really cool
feature that comes out

00:36:20.050 --> 00:36:22.725
of the box-- and Apigee,
like David and Matt

00:36:22.725 --> 00:36:24.100
mentioned, it has
a lot of things

00:36:24.100 --> 00:36:25.267
that come with the platform.

00:36:25.267 --> 00:36:27.850
But one of the cool things that
I want to show you is and that

00:36:27.850 --> 00:36:29.650
has really helped us
in troubleshooting

00:36:29.650 --> 00:36:34.270
our customers' issues real-time
through the gateway is--

00:36:34.270 --> 00:36:36.160
it's called a trace
functionality.

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:42.405
So I'm going to go in here,
pull up the back-end API.

00:36:49.530 --> 00:36:51.880
And I want to click on
the trace link here.

00:36:51.880 --> 00:36:56.250
So when I say this
API and enable trace,

00:36:56.250 --> 00:36:58.630
I can actually go
and start to trace.

00:36:58.630 --> 00:37:01.050
So at this point, anything
that is coming to this sandbox

00:37:01.050 --> 00:37:03.277
API is going to be recorded,
and it will actually

00:37:03.277 --> 00:37:04.860
show me what's
happening or where it's

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:07.120
failing or is it successful.

00:37:07.120 --> 00:37:10.470
So now I go back here and I'm
going to make the same API

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:12.400
call again from the browser.

00:37:12.400 --> 00:37:17.335
So this time it should actually
show me the entire trace.

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:33.110
This is really cool
because what it

00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:35.990
shows me here is this is
where the request came

00:37:35.990 --> 00:37:38.960
into the back-end
Apigee gateway.

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:40.650
I can click on this.

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:44.390
It shows me all the headers
that came into the gateway.

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:46.940
If I go down, it shows
me the entire payload

00:37:46.940 --> 00:37:49.250
that was sent from the
customer or the developer.

00:37:49.250 --> 00:37:51.083
In this case, I was the
one who was actually

00:37:51.083 --> 00:37:52.950
sending it from the browser.

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:54.680
Shows you all the data here.

00:37:54.680 --> 00:37:56.970
And then it shows you
a list of policies.

00:37:56.970 --> 00:37:58.700
So we have a lot of
security policies

00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:00.920
that we execute
against these APIs.

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:02.140
So these are the policies.

00:38:02.140 --> 00:38:04.460
It's showing me that it's
executing all the security

00:38:04.460 --> 00:38:05.300
policies.

00:38:05.300 --> 00:38:06.590
We have standard policies.

00:38:06.590 --> 00:38:10.460
We have Spike Arrest, rate
monitoring, quarter APIs,

00:38:10.460 --> 00:38:13.550
IP whitelisting, and
pretty much all these APIs.

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:16.430
And you can actually click
on these different policies.

00:38:16.430 --> 00:38:17.883
You can get details
of how long it

00:38:17.883 --> 00:38:20.300
took to execute these policies
if you want to go and tweak

00:38:20.300 --> 00:38:21.590
these policies.

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:24.440
And then once a
policy are passed,

00:38:24.440 --> 00:38:29.000
the request goes to the
back-end sandbox environment.

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:31.160
And this is where, again,
it shows the same thing.

00:38:31.160 --> 00:38:32.910
It'll show you all the
headers and payload

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:35.070
that was sent to the back-end.

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:36.020
This is the response.

00:38:36.020 --> 00:38:40.760
It shows you what came back
from the target endpoint, which

00:38:40.760 --> 00:38:42.320
is a sandbox endpoint.

00:38:42.320 --> 00:38:46.420
And then this is what is
the response that actually

00:38:46.420 --> 00:38:47.640
